{"qid":"Q948","names":["Republic of Tunisia"],"title":"Republic of Tunisia","endonym":{"native":"Tunisia","lang":"en"},"capsule":"The Republic of Tunisia gained independence from France on 20 March 1956 under Habib Bourguiba, who abolished the Husainid bey's monarchy in 1957 and ruled as president until a bloodless palace coup by Zine El Abidine Ben Ali in 1987. Ben Ali's 23-year authoritarian government was ended by the Jasmine Revolution in January 2011, making Tunisia the catalyst and initial success story of the Arab Spring; the country adopted a progressive constitution in 2014 establishing a parliamentary republic. The democratic transition eroded under President Kais Saied, who in 2021 suspended parliament, and a new presidential constitution in 2022 concentrated power in the executive.","attestation":"Boundaries from Seshat Cliopatria (Bennett et al., Scientific Data 2025; CC BY 4.0), digitized from standard historical atlases at ~0.07° resolution. Tunisia's post-independence territory is precisely delimited by internationally recognized borders; the 1956 boundary inherited from the French Protectorate is uncontested except for minor maritime delineation with Libya.","attestationLinks":[{"label":"Cliopatria dataset (Seshat, CC BY 4.0)","url":"https://github.com/Seshat-Global-History-Databank/cliopatria"},{"label":"Bennett et al. 2025, Scientific Data","url":"https://www.nature.com/articles/s41597-025-04516-9"}],"citations":[{"label":"Wikipedia: Tunisia","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tunisia"},{"label":"Britannica: Tunisia","url":"https://www.britannica.com/place/Tunisia"},{"label":"Wikipedia: Tunisian Revolution","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Tunisian_Revolution"}],"britannica":"place/Tunisia","cowCode":null,"wikipedia":"Tunisia","rulers":[{"qid":"Q943661","name":"Muhammad VIII al-Amin","start":1943,"end":1957,"image":"Mohamed Lamine Bey.jpg","imageUrl":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/Special:FilePath/Mohamed%20Lamine%20Bey.jpg?width=320","